   262	point somewhere on `L`.  The F5 curve is smooth away from `p_0`, Section 5
   263	excludes an ADE point there, and `p_0` is smooth in the present case.  Hence
   264	the actual fibre is `B_1`, not a larger fibre carrying a hidden root tree.
   265	
   266	### 3.2 Singular `p_0`
   267	
   268	Retain, during the contraction of Section 2, the weight-one exceptional
   269	component `C_S` met by `S`.  Let `h_S` be its coefficient in the exceptional
   270	cycle of `r^*H`.  All other exceptional components contract to points on
   271	the `F_2` endpoint, while `C_S` pushes to the fibre `F`; hence that cycle
   272	pushes forward to `h_S F`.  Comparing
   273	
   274	```text
   275	A=L+S+T+Z_H
   276	```
   277	
   278	with (0.3), (2.2), and (3.1) yields
   279	
   280	```text
   281	b=5-h_S,
   282	T=S_0+(5-h_S)F-sum_(i in I)E_i,
   283	l notin I,       |I|=2(5-h_S),                        (3.3)
   284	
   285	Z_H=h_S F-2E_l-sum_(i notin I, i!=l)E_i.              (3.4)
   286	```
   287	
   288	The expression (3.4) is in the orthogonal total-transform basis; it is not
   289	a claim that the actual exceptional cycle is supported on the displayed
   290	individual `E_i`.
   291	
   292	Since the blowup centres avoid `S_0`, (3.1) also gives
   293	
   294	```text
   295	S.T=3-h_S.                                            (3.5)
   296	```
   297	
   298	Combining (1.7) and (3.5),
   299	
   300	```text
   301	q=6: h_S=2,  T=S_0+3F-sum_(i in I_6)E_i, |I_6|=6;
   302	q=8: h_S=3,  T=S_0+2F-sum_(i in I_4)E_i, |I_4|=4.      (3.6)
   303	```
   304	
   305	Equivalently, the two residual exceptional-cycle classes are
   306	
   307	```text
   308	q=6: Z_H=2F-2E_l-E_j-E_k;
   309	q=8: Z_H=3F-2E_l-E_j-E_k-E_u-E_v,                     (3.7)
   310	```
   311	
   312	where the displayed indices are the complements of `I` and `l`.  Their
   313	squares are respectively `-6` and `-8`, as required by `Z_H^2=-q`.
   314	
   315	The retained root has class
   316	
   317	```text
   318	C_S=F-E_i-E_j.                                       (3.8)
   319	```
   320	
   321	for the two (possibly successive infinitely-near) centres lying on its
   322	transform.  Positivity of `T.C_S=1-1_(i in I)-1_(j in I)` gives the exact
   323	proximity test
   324	
   325	```text
   326	T meets C_S:          i,j notin I;
   327	T misses C_S:         exactly one of i,j lies in I.   (3.9)
   328	```
   329	
   330	The impossible alternative with both indices in `I` would give negative
   331	intersection.  Likewise the root adjacent to `L=E_l` has coefficient `-1`
   332	in coordinate `l`; this is the total-basis version of its physical edge to
   333	`L`.  Equations (3.3), (3.8), and (3.9), together with the chronological
   334	proximity inequalities for the nine blowups, are binding.  Merely choosing
   335	subsets `I` does not prove effectivity.
   336	
   337	## 4. Physical carrier classification at a singular F5 point
   338	
   339	Fix the standard root numbering displayed in (2.1), and fix `L` rather than
   340	quotienting it by an abstract diagram automorphism.  Each F5 branch has one
   341	unit transverse exceptional contact.  For `q=6`, (1.7) says that `S` and
   342	`T` use the same physical smooth point of one exceptional component; the
   343	`L` contact is a different physical point.  For `q=8`, all three contacts
   344	are physically distinct.  This converts the provisional local Cartan rows
   345	into the following exact marked-fibre table.
   346	
   347	| `q` | local row and actual fibre | physical carrier assignment | rank at `p_0` | verdict |
   348	|---:|---|---|---:|---|
   349	| 6 | `A_2`, `B_3`, `a=3e_1` | `L` meets `E_1`; `S,T` share another point of `E_1` | 2 | necessary survivor |
   350	| 6 | `A_r`, `B_(r+1)`, `3<=r<=8` | the singleton in `2e_1+e_(r-1)` is not the endpoint forced by `L` | `r` | killed; in particular `B_4/A_3` dies |
   351	| 6 | `A_3`, `U_3`, `a=2e_1+e_2` | `L` meets central vertex `2`; `S,T` share a point on spin `1` | 3 | necessary survivor |
   352	| 6 | `D_4`, `U_4`, `a=e_1+e_3+e_4` | `L` meets `1`; `S,T` meet the two distinct spins `3,4` | 4 | killed because `S.T=0`, not `1` |
   353	| 8 | `A_r`, `B_(r+1)`, `4<=r<=8`, `a=e_1+e_2+e_(r-2)` | `L` meets endpoint `1`; `S,T` meet `2,r-2` at distinct points | `r` | necessary survivors `B_5` through `B_9` |
   354	| 8 | `D_5`, `U_5`, `a=e_1+2e_4` | `L` meets `1`; `S,T` use distinct points of one spin | 5 | necessary survivor |
   355	| 8 | `D_6`, `U_6`, `a=e_1+e_5+e_6` | `L` meets `1`; `S,T` use the two spin vertices | 6 | necessary survivor |
   356	
   357	For `A_4`, the two section contacts in the fifth row are distinct physical
   358	points on the same vertex `2`.  A formal coefficient two is not itself a
   359	single germ.  The reason `B_4/A_3` and `U_3/A_3` differ is precisely the
   360	marked nonexceptional carrier: `L` is forced to an endpoint in `B_4` but to
   361	the central vertex in `U_3`.  The reason `D_4` dies is the residual physical
   362	intersection (1.7), not a Cartan congruence.
   363	
   364	The table proves (0.5).  It is not an analytic realization theorem.  In
   365	particular, the three prescribed smooth attachment points, the total-basis
   366	proximity history, and an incidence equation still have to coexist.
   367	
   368	At `p_0`, finiteness near `H` rules out an additional nonexceptional
   369	`A`-null `(-2)` component in the same connected fibre tree: its image would
   370	be a positive-dimensional `pi`-fibre passing through `H`.  Thus the root
   371	tree used in the table is the whole `B_s/U_s` root block.  Away from `p_0`,
   372	the present hypotheses are not globally projectively finite.  Abstract
   373	`D9` roots must therefore still be separated from actual effective
   374	exceptional curves and possible nonfinite fibre carriers.
   375	
   376	One useful exact subfilter survives.  If every `(-2)` vertex of a `U_s`
   377	block away from `p_0` is known to be `r`-exceptional, a section must meet a
   378	weight-one spin vertex and hence would pass through an ADE point on the
   379	otherwise smooth F5 boundary.  That is impossible.  Therefore there is no
   380	additional **all-exceptional** `U_s` block away from `p_0`.  This does not
   381	discard an incompletely identified/nonfinite fibre merely because its
   382	abstract root signature is tagged `U_s`.
   383	
   384	## 5. The unconditional reduced-infinity energy
   385	
   386	There is no ADE point of `X` on `H` away from `p_0`.  Indeed the local
   387	crepant genus identity at a point of reduced `H` is
   388	
   389	```text
   390	delta_p(H)=h^t a/2+sum_(q over p)delta_q(H').          (5.1)
   391	```
   392	
   393	Its right side is positive at an ADE point, whereas every point of the F5
   394	curve away from `p_0` is smooth.
   395	
   396	Globally put
   397	
   398	```text
   399	D_H=Supp(r^*H)=H'+sum E,
   400	W_H=r^*H-D_H=sum(h_i-1)E_i.                            (5.2)
   401	```
   402	
   403	The reduced divisor `D_H` is connected.  Since `A` and `K_Xtilde` are
   404	orthogonal to every Du Val exceptional curve and `p_a(A)=2`, adjunction
   405	gives
   406	
   407	```text
   408	p_a(D_H)
   409	 =2-(1/2)sum_trees (h-1)^t C(h-1)>=0,
   410	
   411	sum_trees (h-1)^t C(h-1)<=4.                          (5.3)
   412	```
   413	
   414	This cap is unconditional in the present reduced-`H` scope.  At a connected
   415	F5 tree, `sum a_i=3` and `1^tC1=2`, so its energy is
   416	
   417	```text
   418	(h-1)^tC(h-1)=h^t a-2sum a_i+2=q-4.                   (5.4)
   419	```
   420	
   421	Thus a `q=6` tag consumes two units and a `q=8` tag consumes all four.  Since
   422	there is no other ADE point on `H`, these are the full global `H` energies,
   423	not per-point budgets.
   424	
   425	## 6. Affine `A`-null carriers
   426	
   427	Let `Z` be a nonexceptional curve contracted by `pi` to an affine
   428	coefficient basepoint.  In the incidence it is the full source line over
   429	that target point, so
   430	
   431	```text
   432	A.Z=0,       B.Z=1.
   433	```
   434	
   435	Its strict transform is a smooth rational curve.  Adjunction gives
   436	`Z^2=-3`, and finiteness near `H` makes it disjoint from `H`, hence from
   437	`S_0`.  In the marking (0.3) every such curve has the necessary class
   438	
   439	```text
   440	Z_J=S_0+2F-sum_(j in J)E_j,       |J|=5.              (6.1)
   441	```
   442	
   443	The entries are zero or one because the successive transforms of a smooth
   444	curve have multiplicity one at every centre they contain.
   445	
   446	For a fixed marked F5 state, disjointness from `L` and `T` gives:
   447	
   448	| state | exact subset equations | raw coordinate candidates |
   449	|---|---|---:|
   450	| smooth `p_0` | `l in J`; equivalently choose four of the other eight | 70 |
   451	| singular `q=6` | `l notin J`, `|J cap I_6|=3` | 20 |
   452	| singular `q=8` | `l notin J`, `|J cap I_4|=2` | 24 |
   453	
   454	These counts precede chronological proximity and effectivity.  Distinct
   455	contracted curves lie over distinct target points and hence are disjoint.
   456	But
   457	
   458	```text
   459	Z_J.Z_K=2-|J cap K|,                                  (6.2)
   460	```
   461	
   462	so coexistence requires `|J cap K|=2`; three such five-subsets cannot coexist
   463	by inclusion-exclusion.  In the singular rows there are respectively `10`
   464	and `36` raw compatible unordered pairs.  Again these are numerical
   465	signatures, not constructed coefficient basepoints.
   466	
   467	Every such `Z_J` is a component of ramification because the differential of
   468	`pi` drops rank generically along a contracted curve.  Its Cartier
   469	multiplicity is **not** controlled by height-one inertia in the target: its
   470	image has codimension two.  This is the central scope firewall for the
   471	global different.
   472	
   473	## 7. Exact global ramification class system
   474	
   475	In the `F_2` marking,
   476	
   477	```text
   478	r^*R_X=2A+B=4S_0+11F-2sum_iE_i.                       (7.1)
   479	```
   480	
   481	Separate the strict nonexceptional ramification carriers into:
   482	
   483	```text
   484	C_j:   A.C_j>0, mapping generically to a target divisor;
   485	Z_k:   A.Z_k=0, contracted to an affine target point;
   486	M:     sum m_alpha E_alpha, the actual Du Val exceptional cycle.
   487	```
   488	
   489	At the generic point of a `C_j`, the charged fixed-sheet cubic theorem gives
   490	tame inertia `(2,1)`, so its different coefficient is one.  This applies to
   491	every noncontracted prime: no projective prime can hide wholly at infinity,
   492	because `pi^*(L_infinity)=H` and `H` shares no component with `R_X`.
   493	Write the uncontrolled coefficient of `Z_k` as `nu_k>=1`.  Then the binding
   494	class and degree equations are
   495	
   496	```text
   497	sum_j C_j+sum_k nu_k Z_k+M
   498	     =4S_0+11F-2sum_iE_i,                              (7.2)
   499	
   500	sum_j A.C_j=8,
   501	sum_j B.C_j+sum_k nu_k=4.                              (7.3)
   502	```
   503	
   504	At every ADE tree, `M` has positive integral coefficient vector `m` and
   505	strict-transform intersection vector `n=Cm>=0`.  The total-basis expansion
   506	of `M` may contain an `F` term; it must not be replaced by a sum of the
   507	orthogonal symbols `E_i` with the same coefficients.
   508	
   509	For a horizontal rational carrier, write
   510	
   511	```text
   512	C=dS_0+bF-sum_i mu_iE_i,       d=B.C>=1.               (7.4)
   513	```
   514	
   515	The multiplicities are nonnegative and obey the chronological proximity
   516	inequalities.  If `a=A.C`, then
   517	
   518	```text
   519	a=d+2b-sum_i mu_i,
   520	C^2=-2d^2+2db-sum_i mu_i^2,
   521	delta(C)=1+(C^2-a+d)/2 in Z_(>=0).                     (7.5)
   522	```
   523	
   524	The last equality uses the rational-forest theorem, which makes the
   525	normalization of every reduced boundary carrier rational.  Nonexceptional
   526	vertical fibre carriers have `d=0` and must instead be read directly from
   527	the marked `B_s/U_s` diagrams.
   528	
   529	If `p_0` is smooth, the charged local calculation gives two distinct
   530	nonexceptional ramification primes `C_3,C_5` with
   531	
   532	```text
   533	A.C_3=3,       A.C_5=5.                               (7.6)
   534	```
   535	
   536	They are distinct because placing the two local normalization branches on
   537	one global prime creates a rational-forest cycle.  Equation (7.3) then says
   538	that every other nonexceptional ramification prime is one of the contracted
   539	`Z_k`.  Hence the exact smooth-`p_0` decomposition is
   540	
   541	```text
   542	r^*R_X=C_3+C_5+sum_k nu_k Z_k+M.                      (7.7)
   543	```
   544	
   545	This does not import the old smooth-surface lattice closure: `X` may still
   546	have affine ADE points and affine coefficient basepoints.
   547	
   548	If `p_0` is singular, the strict local different has not yet been factored.
   549	The positive integers `A.C_j` form a partition of eight, all their boundary
   550	contact is over `p_0`, and (7.2)--(7.5) are the exact finite threat system.
   551	No analogue of the smooth `3+5` split is asserted.
   552	
   553	## 8. Proper residual fibre identities, localization, and support genus
   554	
   555	The raw `B.R_X=4` cap cannot be charged against a fibre that is itself a
   556	strict carrier.  Let an actual scheme fibre be
   557	
   558	```text
   559	F_t=Gamma+sum_i beta_iE_i,                             (8.1)
   560	```
   561	
   562	where `Gamma` is its scheme-weighted nonexceptional part.  Let `V` be the
   563	sum of all nonexceptional fibre components common with the strict
   564	ramification, put `v_i=V.E_i`, and write `R_o=R_str-V`.  Total-fibre
   565	orthogonality gives `F_t.V=F_t.M=0`, and properness after removing every
   566	common carrier gives the exact identity
   567	
   568	```text
   569	4=F_t.r^*R_X=Gamma.R_o+beta^t(n-v),
   570	beta^t(n-v)<=4.                                       (8.2)
   571	```
   572	
   573	All terms in the displayed decomposition are nonnegative.  It is
   574	`n-v`, not the formal `n`, that consumes the residual fibre budget.
   575	
   576	At the F5 fibre, put `ell_i=L.E_i`.  Since `L` is the unique common
   577	nonexceptional fibre component of `r^*H`, the parallel identity is
   578	
   579	```text
   580	2=F_0.r^*H=Gamma.(S+T)+beta^t(a-ell).                 (8.3)
   581	```
   582	
   583	For singular `p_0`, the first term is zero and the two section contacts have
   584	weight one, so (8.3) is saturated as `2=0+2`.  For smooth `p_0`, there is no
   585	exceptional term and the two intersections with `L` give `2=2+0`.  These
   586	are shared budgets on the actual fibre, not independent per-singularity
   587	caps.
   588	
   589	There is a separate proper target-image budget.  For a target point `q`,
   590	choose a generic target line through `q` which is not the image of a strict
   591	ramification carrier.  If `ell^(p)` is its positive exceptional valuation
   592	vector at each singular point `p` over `q`, total-pullback orthogonality
   593	leaves exactly
   594	
   595	```text
   596	8=r^*M_q.r^*R_X
   597	 =M_q'.R_str+sum_(p over q) (ell^(p))^t n^(p).         (8.4)
   598	```
   599	
   600	The proper strict term is nonnegative and every entry of every `ell^(p)` is
   601	at least one.  Consequently
   602	
   603	```text
   604	sum_(p over q) sum_i n_i^(p)<=8.                       (8.5)
   605	```
   606	
   607	This is shared by singularities with the same target image.  It is neither
   608	a per-point copy of eight nor the fibre budget (8.2).  Common target-image
   609	carriers have been excluded by the generic choice of line.
   610	
   611	Let `Y=X minus H` and `U=Y minus Supp(R_X)`.  Dominance by the affine source
   612	gives
   613	
   614	```text
   615	O(Y)^*=O(U)^*=C^*.
   616	```
   617	
   618	The divisor localization sequence therefore injects the free group on all
   619	reduced nonexceptional ramification primes into `Cl(Y)`.  Consequently any
   620	relation on `Xtilde`
   621	
   622	```text
   623	sum_j q_j C_j+sum_k q_k Z_k ~lin cA+(exceptional divisor) (8.6)
   624	```
   625	
   626	with a nonzero vector of coefficients is impossible after pushdown and
   627	restriction to `Y`.  Cartier multiplicities in (7.2) are irrelevant to this
   628	reduced-prime independence test.
   629	
   630	There is also an exact connected-support formula.  Put
   631	
   632	```text
   633	D_R=Supp(r^*R_X),
   634	W_R=r^*R_X-D_R
   635	   =sum_k(nu_k-1)Z_k+sum_alpha(m_alpha-1)E_alpha.       (8.7)
   636	```
   637	
   638	The reduced support `D_R` is connected and `p_a(r^*R_X)=9`.  Since
   639	
   640	```text
   641	r^*R_X.Z_k=K_Xtilde.Z_k=1,
   642	r^*R_X.E_alpha=K_Xtilde.E_alpha=0,
   643	```
   644	
   645	adjunction gives the unconditional equality
   646	
   647	```text
   648	p_a(D_R)
   649	 =9+(W_R^2-3sum_k(nu_k-1))/2>=0.                      (8.8)
   650	```
   651	
   652	Only in the additional subcell `nu_k=1` for every contracted carrier does
   653	(8.8) reduce to
   654	
   655	```text
   656	sum_trees (m-1)^t C(m-1)<=18.                         (8.9)
   657	```
   658	
   659	There is no theorem in the charged scope forcing that subcell.  In
   660	particular, fixed-sheet divisorial inertia does not control a curve mapped
   661	to a target point, so (8.9) is **conditional**.
   662	
   663	Finally, the reduced resolved first-leg boundary is a rational forest.
   664	Connected total transforms of `H` and `Supp(R_X)` may have only one joining
   665	cluster.  Within `Supp(R_X)`, an affine ADE tree or an `A`-null carrier cannot
   666	give a second path between already-connected carrier subgraphs.  Physical
   667	attachment points must first be grouped into clusters; a formal vector
   668	entry, a root vertex, and a physical germ are three different objects.
   669	This connector test and the class-group test (8.6) are exact eliminators,
   670	but neither is a converse effectivity criterion.
   671	
   672	## 9. Global finite state and exact remaining gap
   673	
   674	A global necessary state is now specified by the following finite data.
   675	
   676	1. Choose the smooth `B_1` state (0.4), or one of the nine marked singular
   677	   tags (0.5), including its subset `I`, total-basis root classes, physical
   678	   attachment partition, and proximity history.
   679	2. Allocate the remaining blowup coordinates among actual affine fibre
   680	   blocks.  Retain their source-fibre labels, target-image labels, effective
   681	   exceptional curves, and local pairs `(m,n)`; do not replace this layer by
   682	   an unmarked `W(D9)` root orbit.
   683	3. Add zero, one, or two candidates (6.1), with their multiplicities `nu`
   684	   and proximity histories.
   685	4. Solve (7.2)--(7.5), the proper residual identities (8.2)--(8.5), the
   686	   localization test (8.6), support genus (8.8), and the physical
   687	   rational-forest connector test.
   688	
   689	The state space is finite.  There are nine blowups; (8.5) bounds every local
   690	`n` and hence every `m=C^(-1)n`; (7.3) bounds the horizontal and contracted
   691	carrier degrees; and pushing (7.2) to `F_2` bounds every nonnegative fibre
   692	coefficient by eleven.  Equations (7.3)--(7.5) then bound all centre
   693	multiplicities.  No heavy enumeration is justified yet: the
   694	singular-`p_0` local different partition and the multiplicities `nu_k` are
   695	the two missing geometric inputs, and enumerating before they are known
   696	would only multiply nonrealizable lattice signatures.
   697	
   698	For later filters, expose the two coarse invariants
   699	
   700	```text
   701	rho = total rank of every Du Val tree on X, including affine trees;
   702	k   = number of distinct reduced nonexceptional ramification primes,
   703	      including every affine contracted Z_k regardless of nu_k. (9.1)
   704	```
   705	
   706	The present theorem gives `k>=2` in the smooth-`p_0` state and `k>=1` in
   707	every singular state.  The local contribution to `rho` is respectively
   708	
   709	```text
   710	state:       smooth A2 A3 A4 A5 A6 A7 A8 D5 D6
   711	rho_p0:        0    2  3  4  5  6  7  8  5  6.        (9.2)
   712	```
   713	
   714	### Review-gated out-of-basis successor
   715	
   716	A later producer announced under prefix `b0f4d09c`, not present on the
   717	frozen basis and still under review, proposes an Euler/`A^1`-ruling cap
   718	
   719	```text
   720	rho+s+k<=11,
   721	```
   722	
   723	where `s=3` for F5, hence `rho+k<=8`; its additional `P1`-base branch would
   724	give `rho+k<=7`.  This packet does not charge either inequality.  If the
   725	review promotes them, (9.2) makes the interface immediate: `A8` dies because
   726	`k>=1`, and every rank-at-least-seven state dies when `k>=2`.  Equality has
   727	the extra irreducible-reduced-fibre condition in that new `A^1` ruling, not in
   728	the conic `B`-fibration used here.
   729	
   730	The exact remaining gap is therefore not an abstract `D9` orbit count.  It
   731	is the singular-F5 local different together with effectivity/proximity and
   732	the possible higher multiplicity of affine Stein-contracted carriers.  This
   733	packet proves no realization or exclusion of the nine tags, no global
   734	projective finiteness, no unconditional ramification-energy cap, no
   735	quadratic basis-minimization theorem, no higher-degree block result, no map
   736	or counterexample, and no JC2 conclusion.  Normal ADE triage outside this
   737	declared reduced F5 cell remains separate.
