    89	```text
    90	J={l} union K,       K subset U,       |U|=8, |K|=4.  (2.1)
    91	```
    92	
    93	There are `binom(8,4)=70` candidates.  For two candidates, (1.3) becomes
    94	`|K cap K'|=1`.  For fixed `K`, choose the common element in four ways and
    95	the other three elements of `K'` from the four-element complement in four
    96	ways.  Hence the compatible unordered-pair count is
    97	
    98	```text
    99	70*(4*4)/2=560.                                       (2.2)
   100	```
   101	
   102	Three compatible candidates cannot occur.  Directly in the original
   103	nine-set, all three `J` contain `l`, so their triple intersection has size
   104	at least one.  If every pair has intersection two, inclusion-exclusion
   105	would give
   106	
   107	```text
   108	|J_1 union J_2 union J_3|
   109	 =15-6+|J_1 cap J_2 cap J_3|>=10,                     (2.3)
   110	```
   111	
   112	which is impossible in a nine-set.  This is the missing hypothesis in the
   113	original argument and the exact reason the counterexample (0.1), whose
