   113	forest.
   114	
   115	In a forest, two connected subgraphs cannot have two separated joining
   116	sites.  Equivalently, after contracting the internal tree in each total
   117	transform, two physical incidences give parallel edges; blowups only
   118	subdivide those edges or add pendant vertices.  Shared Du Val exceptional
   119	trunks do not change this: if two distinct points are singular on `X`, the
   120	two exceptional trees are disjoint joining sites.  A common strict carrier
   121	would be the only escape, and the preceding factor argument excludes one
   122	between `H` and `R_X` in the present reduced finite scope.
   123	
   124	It follows that (1.1) can have only one physical **support site**.  This does
   125	not assert that the reduced ramification has only one analytic branch there:
   126	distinct ramification carrier primes can form a star at one site, exactly as
   127	in the F5 local-different problem.  What is forced here is that the intrinsic
   128	projection `H->L_infinity` has only one physical critical site.
