   170	## 2. Fibre normal form
   171	
   172	Let `C` be a final smooth rational component of a fibre affected by the nine
   173	blowups.  Since `B.C=0`, adjunction and (0.1) give
   174	
   175	```text
   176	-2=(K_Xtilde+C).C=-A.C+C^2,
   177	A.C=C^2+2.                                             (2.1)
   178	```
   179	
   180	The class `A` is nef.  Hence `C^2>=-2`.  The first blowup changes the original
   181	square-zero fibre component to `-1` and creates a `-1` exceptional component;
   182	every later component is born with square `-1`.  Consequently every component
   183	of an affected final fibre has square `-1` or `-2`.
   184	
   185	Write the scheme fibre as
   186	
   187	```text
   188	F_t=sum_v beta_v C_v.                                  (2.2)
   189	```
   190	
   191	Using `A.B=2` and (2.1),
   192	
   193	```text
   194	2=A.F_t=sum_(C_v^2=-1) beta_v.                         (2.3)
   195	```
   196	
   197	No component already at `-2` can contain a later blowup centre.  After the
   198	first blowup on a fresh fibre, the unique new edge has two `-1` endpoints.
   199	Every smooth blowup thereafter changes its chosen `-1` to `-2` and appends a
   200	new `-1`; every other edge has a frozen `-2` endpoint.  Therefore a node
   201	blowup, if any, is uniquely the second blowup on that fibre.  There can be
   202	at most one node blowup per fibre.
   203	
   204	This gives exactly two blocks using `s` blowups.
   205	
   206	### 2.1 Smooth-only block `B_s`
   207	
   208	The reduced fibre is the chain
   209	
   210	```text
   211	(-1)-(-2)-...-(-2)-(-1),       beta=(1,...,1),           (2.4)
   212	```
   213	
   214	with `s-1` internal `-2` vertices.  They form `A_(s-1)`.  For `s=1` there
   215	is no root; this is the unused coordinate `u` in (0.4).  If the original
   216	square-zero component is marked, its position modulo reversal has
   217	`floor(s/2)+1` possibilities.  The unmarked tree forgets that position.
