    54	   directly to `b=p`: if all components were inside `R`, the whole principal
    55	   divisor would be missed.  If the primes above `(p)` have ramification and
    56	   residue degrees `(e_i,f_i)`, geometric inertia fixes exactly
    57	   `sum_(e_i=1) f_i` sheets, and this sum is at least one.
    58	3. **No proper Galois quotient.**  The extension `K/C(f,g)` is not Galois.
    59	   Otherwise transitivity of the Galois group on primes over a height-one
    60	   prime makes every `e_i` equal.  A component of the nonempty branch support
    61	   supplies an `e_i>1`, while item 2 supplies an `e_i=1`, a contradiction.
    62	   Normality, not smoothness of `Y`, is enough for the height-one DVR
    63	   argument; characteristic zero supplies separable residue extensions and
    64	   tame divisorial ramification.
    65	4. **No two-block system.**  In particular `d2!=2`, because every quadratic
    66	   extension in characteristic zero is Galois.  In monodromy notation
    67	   `H subset J subset G`, this says `[G:J]!=2` and `J` is not normal in `G`.
    68	   It says nothing about the block size `[J:H]`; in particular it does not
    69	   exclude `d1=2`.
    70	5. **Structural corollaries.**  `B_K` is nonfactorial and nonmonogenic over
    71	   `C[f,g]`.  The rank-two direct proof writes a hypothetical degree-two
    72	   algebra globally as `A[w]/(w^2-h)`; its ramification set is the nonempty
    73	   principal divisor `V_Y(w)`, contradicting item 1.  This second proof is
    74	   independent of the Galois-uniformity argument, but shares the same source-
    75	   unit mechanism and is not advertised as logically independent of item 1.
    76	
    77	“Branch” and “discriminant” agree here only at the level of support.  No
    78	divisor multiplicities are identified.  A fixed sheet of `g2` is a fixed
    79	block of the full map; it is not necessarily a single source sheet.
    80	
    81	## 2. Independently checked strengthenings
    82	
    83	The reviewer derived the following two claims.  The coordinator checked
    84	their proofs directly against the promoted sandwich; they are included with
    85	their exact global scope.
    86	
    87	**Class-lattice injection.**  Let `Z=Y minus g1(A^2)`.  The free abelian
    88	group on the codimension-one components of `Z` injects into `Cl(Y)`.  If a
    89	divisor supported in `Z` were `div(b)`, then both `b` and `b^{-1}` would be
    90	regular on `g1(A^2)`.  Their pullbacks would be mutually inverse polynomials
    91	on `A^2`, hence scalars; the function-field inclusion then makes `b` scalar.
    92	Thus every ramification-component class has infinite order and `Cl(Y)` is
    93	infinite whenever `R` is nonempty.
    94	
    95	**Nonprincipal different.**  The relative dual
    96	
